WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
The ML lifecycle.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:06.067
Here begins MLOps, and it starts with one mindset shift.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:12.400
In classic software, shipping is the finish line, because code does not rot by itself.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.912
A model is different.

5
00:00:13.912 --> 00:00:16.567
It froze a snapshot of the world into weights.

6
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:23.767
And the world keeps changing underneath it: sensors get recalibrated, behavior shifts, upstream schemas evolve.

7
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:26.233
Here is the whole loop.

8
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:29.233
We start with data, versioned by DVC.

9
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:33.133
Then training, tracked by MLflow or Weights and Biases.

10
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.000
Then evaluation, the gate before release.

11
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.633
Then deployment, through the registry and CI/CD.

12
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:43.833
Then monitoring, where drift alarms fire and send us back to data.

13
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:47.933
Here the loop is written down as pipeline stages.

14
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:49.833
Ingest produces the data.

15
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.100
Train depends on that data and produces a model.

16
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.152
Evaluate and deploy follow.

17
00:00:55.152 --> 00:01:01.833
Monitoring lives in production, outside this graph, and when drift fires, the loop re-enters at ingest.

18
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:05.967
Picture an exoplanet classifier through its first year.

19
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.610
It ships in January.

20
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.700
February brings new data, and March a retrain.

21
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.774
In April the eval gate blocks a regression.

22
00:01:13.774 --> 00:01:15.967
In May, version three is promoted.

23
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:20.029
In August a drift alarm fires, and the loop begins again.

24
00:01:20.029 --> 00:01:21.811
One model, three full laps.

25
00:01:21.811 --> 00:01:22.867
That is the job.

26
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:27.000
The common mistake is treating deployment as the end.

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.367
Instead, every deploy schedules the investigation that leads to the next retrain.

28
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:34.317
Let's recap.

29
00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:36.567
The lifecycle is a loop, not a line.

30
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:40.100
A model freezes the world, and the world keeps changing.

31
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.267
Every tool in MLOps is a station on this loop.

32
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:47.627
So any conversation becomes: which station are we talking about?

33
00:01:47.627 --> 00:01:49.467
Next up: experiment tracking.
